-- Passage rg6-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('23ca879d-994d-53e3-9bbc-185f2367c374', 'rg6-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$When Ice Came from Lakes$q$, $q$Long before kitchens had refrigerators, people still found ways to keep food cold in summer. In many northern towns in the 1800s, the answer came from frozen lakes and ponds. Ice harvesting grew into a large business that changed how families stored food.

The work began in the coldest weeks of winter, once the ice was thick enough to hold workers and horses. Crews first cleared snow from the surface. Then they marked the ice into a grid of large blocks. Workers cut along the lines with long saws and used poles to float the blocks through open channels toward shore.

Next, the blocks had to last until summer. Workers stacked them inside icehouses, which were buildings with thick walls. They packed sawdust or straw between the blocks. This material slowed the flow of heat, so much of the ice stayed frozen for months.

In warm weather, ice wagons carried blocks through town. Families placed them in wooden iceboxes, which were cabinets lined with metal. Milk, butter and meat stayed fresh longer. Some ice was even shipped to warm places far away.

The business did not last forever. In the early 1900s, factories learned to make ice with machines, and later, electric refrigerators arrived in homes. Natural ice could also be dirty, while machine ice was clean and available all year. Within a few decades, most ice harvesting had ended. Still, for about a century, frozen lakes helped people eat fresher food in every season.$q$, 'draft')
