-- Passage rg6-main-idea-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('01ac5ca6-2273-5023-9c6a-497bd7d29aff', 'rg6-main-idea-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Making a Fresh Sheet of Ice$q$, $q$At a hockey game or a public skating session, the ice starts out smooth and glossy. After an hour of skating, though, it is covered in scratches, ruts and loose snow. Rinks keep their ice in good shape through careful building and regular resurfacing.

A rink's ice begins with a cold floor. Pipes run beneath a concrete slab, carrying a very cold liquid that chills the concrete below freezing. Workers then spray thin layers of water onto the slab, letting each layer freeze before adding the next. The lines and team logos are painted onto an early layer and then covered with more ice. In the end, the ice is usually only about an inch thick.

Skating wears this surface down quickly. Sharp blades carve grooves and shave off fine snow. Bumpy, snowy ice is slower and can make skaters stumble.

That is why a resurfacing machine circles the rink between periods or sessions. A blade underneath shaves a thin layer off the top of the ice. A spinning screw collects the shavings and moves them into a storage tank. Next, the machine washes the surface with water to rinse dirt out of the grooves. Finally, a cloth towel at the back spreads a thin layer of warm water. Warm water melts the top of the old ice slightly, so the new layer bonds tightly as it freezes.

The whole process takes about ten to fifteen minutes. When the machine leaves, skaters return to a surface that is almost as smooth as glass. Every fresh sheet of ice is the result of cold engineering and careful timing.$q$, 'draft')
