-- Passage rg6-main-idea-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('12e9d2e3-ab9c-550e-a2a1-4a59438941a0', 'rg6-main-idea-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$From Lump to Bowl$q$, $q$A handmade clay bowl may look simple, but it goes through a long journey before it reaches a table. Potters shape, dry and fire their work in careful stages. Skipping or rushing any stage can ruin the piece.

The process begins with wedging. A potter kneads a lump of wet clay on a table, pressing and folding it many times. Wedging pushes out trapped air bubbles. If air stays inside, it can expand in the heat later and crack the bowl.

Next comes throwing on the potter's wheel. The potter presses the spinning clay into the center of the wheel, a step called centering. Then the potter pushes a thumb into the middle and slowly pulls the walls upward and outward. Wet hands keep the clay from sticking or tearing.

After shaping, the bowl must dry slowly for several days. When it is firm but not yet hard, the potter trims extra clay from the bottom to form a neat base. Then the bowl dries completely until it turns pale and light.

The first trip into the kiln, a very hot oven, is called the bisque firing. It turns soft clay into hard ceramic. The potter then dips the bowl in glaze, a liquid coating made of minerals. In a second firing, which is usually hotter, the glaze melts into a glassy layer that makes the bowl shiny and waterproof.

From start to finish, a single bowl can take two weeks or more. Each step depends on the one before it, so a potter must be patient at every stage.$q$, 'draft')
