-- Passage rg6-main-idea-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dde7c400-2cce-5619-acc1-79347f01f8da', 'rg6-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Snow Shovel Squad$q$, $q$Every winter, the town of Maple Hollow gets several heavy snowstorms. For most families, a snowy morning means an hour of shoveling. For older neighbors and people with injuries, though, a blocked sidewalk can mean being stuck at home for days. Three years ago, a group of students decided to do something about it.

The idea started in Ms. Okafor's sixth-grade class. During a lesson about community service, a student named Rafael Ortiz mentioned that his grandmother had missed a doctor's visit because her front steps were buried. Classmates shared similar stories. Together, the class created the Snow Shovel Squad.

The squad works through a simple sign-up system. Neighbors who need help call the town library and leave their address. Librarians add each request to a list and share it with the squad's adult leaders. After a storm, teams of students and parent volunteers are assigned to nearby houses. Each team clears walkways, steps and the space around mailboxes.

Safety rules are an important part of the program. Students work only in daylight, always with an adult, and take a break every thirty minutes. They are taught to lift small scoops of snow and to bend their knees instead of their backs.

The program has grown each year. In the first winter, the squad helped twelve households. Last winter, more than sixty volunteers cleared paths for over ninety homes. Several neighbors have written thank-you notes, and some now bake treats for the teams.

The squad has changed the students too. Many say they have met neighbors they never knew before. What started as one class project has become a winter tradition that helps the whole town stay connected.$q$, 'draft')
